Metrics for multiclass classification
If there are more than two possible classes1 a generalisation of the discussion above is necessary. Again, the most used metrics are based on the confusion matrix, which now will be of dimension \(k\times k\) where \(k\) is the number of classes.

As before, the diagonal elements are the correctly classified ones. But now, an incorrectly classified element may have been classified wrong in several ways. This is useful information, since it can tell us which classes are harder or easier to separate. Starting from the confusion matrix we can define similar scalar metrics as for the binary case. For each of the \(k\) classes we calculate a \(2\times2\) confusion matrix based on the full confusion matrix:
\[
\begin{align}
    \text{TP}_i &= n_{ii}  \\
    \text{TN}_i &= \sum_{j\neq i} \sum_{h\neq j} n_{jh} \\
    \text{FP}_i &= \sum_{j\neq i} n_{ji} \\
    \text{FN}_i &= \sum_{j\neq i} n_{ij}
\end{align}
\] Using these definition we can for each class (\(i=1,2,\ldots,k\)) calculate accuracy, precision etc. To get an overall measure of the performance we need to average over the individual measures. Two options for averging procedure seems natural, and one have to choose the procedure that fits the problem at hand. With macro-averaging we calculate the arithmetic mean of all classes, \[
\text{Precision (macro)} = \frac{1}{k}\sum\text{Prescision}_i = \frac{1}{k}\sum\frac{\text{TP}_i}{\text{TP}_i + \text{FP}_i}
\] Averaging this way, all classes are given equal weight.
The alternative, called micro-averaging, is to sum over all classes first and then calculate the metric: \[
\text{Precision (micro)} = \frac{\sum\text{TP}_i}{\sum\text{TP}_i + \sum\text{FP}_i}
\] Averaging this way, classes with more instances are given more weight.
